\section*{The exact critical dimension for homogeneous one-phase minimizers}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Critical Bernoulli dimension}: The claimed first nonflat minimizing Bernoulli cone occurs in dimension seven; all homogeneous global solutions that minimize in lower dimensions are flat. Dimensions at most six have smooth local free boundaries, with the announced sharp singular-set bounds in higher dimensions.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Critical Bernoulli dimension}: A first hypothetical nonflat minimizer below dimension seven is reduced to a spherical component in dimension six. Tensor identities and stability produce a flux comparison whose algebraic estimate is verified with exact coefficient data. Removing cutoffs contradicts the spherical configuration; the known nonflat seven-dimensional construction gives the other side and the sharpness consequences.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Critical Bernoulli dimension}: The question requires both flatness through dimension six and a nonflat minimizing cone in dimension seven. Those two sides determine the exact first nonflat dimension; a seven-dimensional example by itself would not establish the whole assertion.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 367 describes Lean coverage for existence of a nonflat homogeneous minimizer in dimension seven; the lower-dimensional flatness and singular-set classifications are separate.

\textit{Critical Bernoulli dimension}: The manuscript links \texttt{BernoulliNonflatInSeven.lean}.

